{"episodes":{"211861":"An incoming shortwave trough brought showers and some thunderstorms to west Texas and southeast New Mexico overnight April 30th and especially during the morning of May 1st. During the morning of the 1st, rainfall became locally heavy at times, which resulting in flash flooding in urban and low-lying areas. Showers largely cleared the area during the afternoon of the 1st as the shortwave moved east of MAF's CWA, taking the best lift and moisture with it.","211862":"An incoming shortwave trough brought showers and some thunderstorms to west Texas and southeast New Mexico overnight April 30th and especially during the morning of May 1st. During the morning of the 1st, rainfall became locally heavy at times, which resulting in flash flooding in urban and low-lying areas. Showers largely cleared the area during the afternoon of the 1st as the shortwave moved east of MAF's CWA, taking the best lift and moisture with it.","212151":"A hailstorm moves through south Houston overnight from the 30th of April into the early morning hours of May 1st.","212152":"Thunderstorms moved through the area, producing strong winds, mainly along the coast.","212532":"Thunderstorms moved through the coastal waters, producing strong winds.","212742":"On May 1, 2026, severe weather developed across South Texas as a shortwave trough moved eastward out of northern Mexico and interacted with a quasi-stationary frontal boundary draped across South Texas and the middle Texas coast. North of the front, a very moist air mass characterized by dewpoints in the lower to middle 70s combined with moderate instability to support widespread showers and thunderstorms. As the upper-level disturbance approached, increasing large-scale ascent and strengthening mid-level winds enhanced deep-layer shear, allowing some storms to become better organized and rotate. Despite extensive cloud cover and ongoing convection, sufficient instability remained in place for stronger updrafts capable of producing large hail. The combination of strengthening shear and organized storm structures also supported isolated severe wind gusts. Additionally, the development of a surface low along the Texas coast locally enhanced low-level wind fields and directional shear near the frontal boundary, creating a favorable environment for a brief, isolated tornado.","212936":"On May 1, 2026, severe weather developed across South Texas as a shortwave trough moved eastward out of northern Mexico and interacted with a quasi-stationary frontal boundary draped across South Texas and the middle Texas coast. North of the front, a very moist air mass characterized by dewpoints in the lower to middle 70s combined with moderate instability to support widespread showers and thunderstorms. The combination of strengthening shear and organized storm structures supported isolated severe wind gusts.","213008":"Thunderstorms developed in the vicinity of a stationary front, and some of these storms produced large hail and damaging wind gusts.","213596":"Thunderstorms developed in the vicinity of a stationary front, and some of these storms produced large hail and damaging wind gusts.","214270":"The river flooding that began from the heavy rainfall in mid April continued into early May for portions of the Rock River."},"events":[{"begin":"2026-05-01T04:30Z","county":"Dimmit","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-05-01T04:30Z","endLat":28.46,"endLon":-99.79,"episode":213008,"id":1333089,"injuries":0,"lat":28.46,"location":"2.53 WNW Asherton","lon":-99.79,"magnitude":2.75,"narrative":"A thunderstorm produced chicken egg to baseball size hail at the White Wing Motel on Hwy 83 northwest of Asherton.","source":"Public","state":"TX","type":"Hail","wfo":"EWX"},{"begin":"2026-05-01T04:31Z","county":"Dimmit","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-05-01T04:31Z","endLat":28.45,"endLon":-99.76,"episode":213008,"id":1333091,"injuries":0,"lat":28.4